## Why This Is Asked
Tests CGO memory ownership, cross-language string handling, and concurrency basics with a concrete data format (JSON). It also touches error propagation across C/Go boundaries.

## Key Concepts
- CGO memory management across Go/C; single-site deallocation
- Passing Go slices to C as C.int* and handling lifetimes
- Returning heap-allocated JSON and translating to Go strings
- Thread-safety when multiple goroutines call into CGO

## Code Example
```javascript
// Header (histogram.h)
char* histogram10(const int* data, size_t n, int* err);

// Minimal C implementation (histogram.c)
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include "histogram.h"

char* histogram10(const int* data, size_t n, int* err) {
  if (!data) { *err = 1; return NULL; }
  int counts[10] = {0};
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    int v = data[i]; if (v >= 0 && v < 10) counts[v]++;
  }
  // allocate small JSON, omit exact sizing for brevity
  char* json = (char*)malloc(128);
  if (!json) { *err = 2; return NULL; }
  snprintf(json, 128, "{\"bins\":[%d,%d,%d,%d,%d,%d,%d,%d,%d,%d]}",
           counts[0],counts[1],counts[2],counts[3],counts[4],
           counts[5],counts[6],counts[7],counts[8],counts[9]);
  *err = 0; return json;
}
```

```javascript
// Go binding (histogram.go)
package main
/*
#include "histogram.h"
#include <stdlib.h>
*/
import "C"
import (
  "errors"
  "unsafe"
)

func ComputeHistogram(data []int) (string, error) {
  if len(data) == 0 {
    return "", errors.New("empty data")
  }
  // allocate C array
  cdata :=(*C.int)(unsafe.Pointer(&data[0]))
  var err C.int
  cstr := C.histogram10(cdata, C.size_t(len(data)), &err)
  if cstr == nil {
    return "", fmt.Errorf("C histogram error: %d", err)
  }
  defer C.free(unsafe.Pointer(cstr))
  return C.GoString(cstr), nil
}
```

